`startdate` marks the local time when an activity was logged. As per the instructions, it should be valid until the next activity is logged. This allows us to put `start` and `end` timepoints to each row.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
events <-
  events |> 
  dplyr::mutate(
    start = as.POSIXct(startdate, tz = "UTC"), #<1>
    status.duration = c(diff(start), NA_real_), #<2> 
    end = case_when( #<3>
              is.na(lead(start)) ~ (start + dhours(6)), #<3>
              .default = start + status.duration #<3>
              ), #<3>
    setting_light = case_when(type == "Bedtime" ~ "Bed", #<4>
                              type == "Non-wear" ~ "Non-wear", #<4>
                              .default = setting_light |> as.character()) |> #<4>
                    factor(levels = #<4>
                           c("Bed", "Indoors", "Mixed", "Outdoors", "Non-wear") #<4>
                           ), #<4>
    record_id = as.numeric(record_id), #<5>
    .by = record_id, #<6>
    .after = startdate) |> 
  left_join(participant_data |> select(device_id, record_id), by = "record_id") |> #<7>
  rename(Id = device_id) |> #<7>
  mutate(Id = factor(Id)) |> #<7>
  semi_join(participant_data, by = "record_id") #<8>
```
:::


1. The start variable is already presend, but it is a `character` string and needs to be converted to a datetime
2. The duration of a status is the difference of consecutive time points. Because the last log entry does not have a lead, we need to add a missing value at the end.
3. For the end, we differentiate between cases where there is no next entry - in those cases, we simply define the length as until the end of data collection. To cover this time span, it is safe to assume a duration of six hours. The end will be automatically capped to the end of the wearable data, when we merge it later on. In cases where there is a next entry, we use the start of the next log entry as an endpoint.
4. Creating a general setting that differentiates between the main states
5. We need to add the `device_id` to the event data, the link is the `record_id`, which needs to be numeric for no. 7
6. All the operations above need to be performed on a by-participant fashion
7. Adding `device_id`. For the merge, it needs to a factor `Id`, which is the grouping variable in `light_data`
8. Removing all `record_id`'s that are not part of `participant_data`

