### Remove non-wear

As we now have logs of non-wear (both during the day and in sleep), we can set those measurements to `NA`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
light_data <-
  light_data |> 
  mutate(LIGHT = case_match(as.character(type),
                            c("Non-wear", "Bedtime") ~ NA_real_,
                            .default = LIGHT))
```
:::


We can check whether we were successful, by summarizing our data depending on `type`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
light_data |> 
  group_by(type) |>
  summarize(mean = mean(LIGHT, na.rm = TRUE))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 4 × 2
  type      mean
  <fct>    <dbl>
1 Wear     1208.
2 Non-wear  NaN 
3 Bedtime   NaN 
4 <NA>      303.
```


:::
:::


This shows us that removing those instances was successful. To close up this use case, we can calculate a few metrics depending on the context with a helper function:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
quick_summaries <- function(variable) {
light_data |> 
  group_by({{ variable }}, .add = TRUE) |>
    durations(LIGHT, show.missing = TRUE, FALSE.as.NA = TRUE) |> #<1>
    ungroup(Id) |>
    summarize_numeric(prefix = "mean_per_participant_") |> #<2>
    extract_metric( #<3>
      light_data |> #<3>
        mutate({{ variable }} := factor({{ variable}})) |> #<3>
        group_by({{ variable }} #<3>
                 ), #<3>
      identifying.colname = {{ variable }}, #<4>
      Datetime.colname = local_time, #<4>
      geo_mean = log_zero_inflated(LIGHT) |> #<5>
             mean(na.rm = TRUE) |> #<5>
             exp_zero_inflated() #<5>
      ) |>
    rename(participants = episodes) |> 
    drop_na() |>
    gt() |>
    fmt_number(geo_mean)
}
```
:::


1. Calculate the duration of every state for each participant
2. Calculate the average duration per state across participants
3. We add the geometric mean to the summary with `extract_metric`, and supply the original dataset, grouped in the same way as our summary is
4. Secondary settings
5. The formula for the geometric mean uses `log_zero_inflated()` and its counterpart `exp_zero_inflated()` to allow for zero-lx values in the dataset

With this helper we can get quick overviews for many aspects:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
quick_summaries(setting_light)
```

